WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.047
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.047 --> 00:00:08.147
There are two kinds of people,
the ones who currently are or

00:00:08.147 --> 00:00:10.405
have been in a complicated relationship.

00:00:10.405 --> 00:00:14.846
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And the ones who don't admit it.

00:00:14.846 --> 00:00:17.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; During your time in this trouble love

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:23.650
affair, you feel anxious, troubled,
you're frustrated, you're not yourself.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.730
Because on one hand,
you can't live without your soul mate,

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:32.320
you remember all the good times,
all the fun things you did together.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:39.360
But on the other hand, you know you're
not okay, there is something unhealthy

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.030
in this relationship that is
making you feel uneasy, troubled,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.280
and at the end of the day, doubtful.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:53.960
And you ask yourself, What can I
do to make this relationship work,

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:55.880
or how do I reach an end?

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.380
Today, I'm going to talk
to you about a love story

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.715
all about my troubled
love affair with plastic.

00:01:03.715 --> 00:01:08.475
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Seriously, it's no joke.

00:01:08.475 --> 00:01:12.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Plastic and

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.830
I have a peculiar relationship.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:19.418
Thankfully, Facebook has a term for
what I'm going through, it's complicated.

00:01:19.418 --> 00:01:22.960
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But why is it complicated?

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.170
Well, on the positive side, there are many
reasons why I fell in love with plastic.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.810
First, I have been working for
more than ten years in the plastic and

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.710
petrochemicals industry.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.790
I owe to plastic my career growth and
my professional development.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.075
&gt;&gt; Actually, thanks to plastic,
I'm here at Stanford.

00:01:41.075 --> 00:01:45.266
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Something worked.

00:01:45.266 --> 00:01:48.259
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Second,

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:50.025
you may not know plastic like I do.

00:01:50.025 --> 00:01:50.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.170
plastic offers many wonderful qualities.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.780
For example in the medical realm it
has helped us to preserve medicine for

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.650
longer, to develop pill capsules,

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:05.370
ask your grandmother,
even to unblock blood vessels.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:11.960
In the automotive sector, plastic weighs
50% less than its substitutes and

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:16.710
has enabled interesting
improvements in fuel efficiency.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:21.373
Also plastic offers interesting advantages
in construction, transportation,

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:24.290
packaging, even agriculture.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.560
And all of this has translated in
jobs creation, economic growth, and

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.030
improvements to our day to day lives.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.460
Actually, plastic is so
wonderful that we use a ton of it!

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:41.720
But not one ton, but
300 million tons of plastic each year.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:47.465
This is equivalent in weight
of 900 Empire State Buildings.

00:02:47.465 --> 00:02:51.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is more than half of the buildings

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.110
in downtown Manhattan.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.290
Yeah, it's a lot of plastic.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:00.058
And this takes me to a negative side,
not you guys.

00:03:00.058 --> 00:03:04.974
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately,

00:03:04.974 --> 00:03:09.845
plastic is not a cheap date,
it is kind of high maintenance.

00:03:09.845 --> 00:03:13.246
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because we waste more than half of

00:03:13.246 --> 00:03:14.715
the plastic we demand.

00:03:14.715 --> 00:03:19.176
This means we're sending
the equivalent of more than 400

00:03:19.176 --> 00:03:23.810
Empire State Buildings to our
landfills and to our oceans, and

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:29.420
we are creating a huge damage to our
ecosystem, wildlife and environment.

00:03:30.503 --> 00:03:38.280
Second, as a rule of thumb, plastic
takes around 400 years to fully degrade.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.574
This means if I waste plastic right
now it's going to stay here for

00:03:42.574 --> 00:03:45.571
my great, great, great, great, great,

00:03:45.571 --> 00:03:50.211
great, great, great, great,
great grandchildren to inherit.

00:03:52.474 --> 00:03:54.238
Is this the legacy I want to leave behind?

00:03:54.238 --> 00:03:56.176
Definitely not.

00:03:58.513 --> 00:04:01.995
And finally, as you may know,
plastic is derived from oil.

00:04:01.995 --> 00:04:06.320
Oil is not cheap, oil is not infinite.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.250
We cannot keep feeding plastics or
have it forever.

00:04:12.829 --> 00:04:15.915
But you see, I am a passionate
person as probably you have seen.

00:04:15.915 --> 00:04:19.070
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I needed to find a way

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:20.569
to make this relationship work.

00:04:22.097 --> 00:04:26.910
And sometimes the solution is staring you
in the face, but you just don't see it.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.630
Well that's what happened to me, and

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.370
I came to realize
the importance of recycling.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.570
Yeah, I think it's a little
bit obvious right?

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:39.910
Unfortunately, recycling
is hard to do all the time.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.110
And my main problem has been commitment.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:45.845
Has this ever happened to
you in a relationship?

00:04:45.845 --> 00:04:47.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Let me explain

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.520
it to you in another way.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.610
It's New Years day, and
you tend to realize that pretty much since

00:04:54.610 --> 00:05:02.440
Thanksgiving, I ate a lot,
partied a lot, maybe drank a lot.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.080
So as a New Year's resolution,
I decide to go to the gym.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.180
And if I were to see this graphically,
it would look something like this.

00:05:12.142 --> 00:05:19.240
The first day,I will rush to the gym at
6:00 AM, it's really crowded by the way.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:24.080
I will work out one hour and a half,
three to four times that week.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.510
And I'll keep doing it the next week and
the next week.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:29.240
But what will happen as time passes on?

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.360
I will have other priorities.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:36.040
So instead of going four to five times
a week I'll go maybe two to three.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:41.570
Instead of going maybe one hour and
a half, I do one hour, maybe 45 minutes,

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.360
because they are all
important things to do.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.710
So my commitment will go down,
something like this.

00:05:52.135 --> 00:05:54.320
Unfortunately, the same thing
happens with recycling.

00:05:58.086 --> 00:06:04.100
And I need to ask myself what can I do to
maintain a strong commitment all the time.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.960
I need to find a way to
make this habit stick.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:14.630
And that's what I understood,
that I needed to differentiate myself, and

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:17.410
I had to go and advocate for recycle.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:21.480
But I'm going to advocate in three ways.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.520
Number one, I'll advocate to myself,

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:29.250
that every time I waste
something I'll think 400 years.

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:32.150
And that's going to become my mantra,

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:36.360
400 years, because that's
the legacy I'm leaving behind.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.700
Number two,
I'll advocate to my peers, family,

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.240
friends, of their legacy
if they don't recycle.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:53.400
Also, find a recycling bin, even if you
have to Just walk a few extra steps.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.300
If there is no recycling bin provide one.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.090
Just by walking these few extra steps or

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:05.750
by stretching your arm to each additional
container you can do a huge difference.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:09.700
Well you may ask yourself

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.500
why is this going to maintain
a strong commitment all the time?

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:21.940
And I needed to do something more,
I needed to compel myself,

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:24.790
push myself beyond my comfort zone.

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.745
And this is how I decided, pretty much,
to advocate to an audience.

00:07:28.745 --> 00:07:30.414
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:44.810
Each and every one of you
are witnesses of my commitment,

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:50.650
